{% render 'section-style', section: section %}
{%- comment -%}
  "Pour it with" — the bottle's food pairings.

  Split out of product-process.liquid so the page can read in the order the
  brief sets: purchase, facts, FOOD, then "still deciding?", then how it is
  made. It used to be the tail of the process section and was lifted above it
  on phones with a :has() reorder — which put the keyboard through the two
  bands in the opposite order to the eye. Two sections in the right order need
  no reordering at all.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- capture code %}{% render 'non-code', product: product %}{% endcapture -%}
{%- assign code = code | strip -%}
{%- assign pairings = product.metafields.custom.pairings.value -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Pairing cards, same override rule as the steps: Pairing blocks win, the
  metafield is the default. Pairings are per-product too — the engine derives
  them — so blocks are for overriding one bottle, not for authoring all nine.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign pair_blocks = section.blocks | where: 'type', 'pairing' -%}
{%- if pair_blocks.size > 0 or pairings != blank -%}
  <div class="non-section" style="border-top:1px solid var(--non-line)">
    <div class="non-mono non-eyebrow">{{ section.settings.heading }}</div>

    {%- comment -%}
      THE PAIRINGS AS PICTURES, on a phone.

      Three bordered cards of prose stacked down a narrow column is three
      paragraphs about food with no food in them — and this is the one band on
      the page whose whole job is to make someone hungry.

      WHERE THE TWO HALVES COME FROM, and why they can be joined.

      The PICTURES are custom.perfect_for_images, which is per-product,
      per-course photography that was already on the products and already being
      rendered — by a section that is not in product.json and has therefore
      never appeared. On NON1 the three files are literally named
      non1-perfect-for-starters, -mains and -dessert.

      The WORDS are custom.pairings, the same approved copy the prose cards
      below print: "Oysters & cured fish", "Chilli and heat", "Goat cheese,
      stone fruit". Starter, main, dessert — the same three courses in the same
      order.

      The first attempt used the images' OWN captions and produced tiles
      reading "Made For Starters" over a bottle's most specific claim about
      food. Technically correct, and it threw away the only interesting thing
      on the band. The pairing titles are what a customer came for.

      IT IS STILL A POSITIONAL JOIN, so it is guarded like one: three images,
      three pairings, or nothing. A mismatched count would put the dessert
      pairing over the starter photograph, which is a factual error about food
      presented as a recommendation. Silently wrong is worse than plainly
      absent, and "absent" here means the prose cards below simply stay.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign pf_images = product.metafields.custom.perfect_for_images.value
      assign pf_ok = false
      # pair_blocks.size == 0 as well: the tiles are labelled from `pairings`,
      # so a product whose cards are coming from editor blocks instead would
      # get tiles saying one thing and expansions saying another.
      if pf_images.size >= 3 and pairings.size >= 3 and pair_blocks.size == 0
        assign pf_ok = true
      endif
    -%}

    {%- if pf_ok -%}
      <div class="non-trip non-trip--pairing" data-non-pair-trip>
        {%- for img in pf_images limit: 3 -%}
          {%- assign pair = pairings[forloop.index0] -%}
          <div class="non-trip__cell">
            {%- comment -%}
              Tapping EXPANDS, it does not navigate and it does not ask the
              Somm. The explanation is two vetted sentences that are already on
              the page — going to the model for a fact we hold would be slower,
              could fail, and could answer differently on two consecutive taps.

              aria-controls points at the matching prose card below, which is
              why those cards are still rendered rather than replaced: they ARE
              the expansion. pairing-triptych.js wires the pair up and owns the
              expanded state.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <button
              type="button"
              class="non-trip__hit"
              data-non-pair-tile
              data-pair-index="{{ forloop.index0 }}"
              aria-expanded="false">
              <span class="visually-hidden">{{ pair.title }}</span>
            </button>

            {%- comment -%}
              The alt describes the photograph. It deliberately does not repeat
              the pairing title, which is rendered as text directly below it.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {{
              img
              | image_url: width: 600, format: 'webp'
              | image_tag:
                loading: 'lazy',
                widths: '200,300,450,600',
                sizes: '33vw',
                class: 'non-trip__img',
                alt: img.alt | default: ''
            }}

            {%- comment -%}
              Not aria-hidden. The whole strip is display:none above the
              breakpoint, so this caption is only ever exposed alongside a live
              control — but the same rule applies as on the homepage triptych:
              a markup attribute cannot know the viewport, and copy that
              disappears for a screen reader is a silent regression. The tile's
              own name carries the pairing; this carries it again.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <div class="non-trip__cap">
              <div class="non-trip__title">{{ pair.title }}</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="non-pairs{% if pf_ok %} non-pairs--has-trip{% endif %}">
      {%- if pair_blocks.size > 0 -%}
        {%- for block in pair_blocks -%}
          <div class="non-pair-card" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
            <div class="non-pair-card__t">{{ block.settings.title }}</div>
            <p class="non-pair-card__b">{{ block.settings.body }}</p>
          </div>
        {%- endfor -%}
      {%- else -%}
        {%- for pair in pairings -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            id and index so a tile above can point at its own card. On desktop
            these attributes are inert — the cards are simply three cards, as
            they have always been.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div
            class="non-pair-card"
            id="non-pair-card-{{ section.id }}-{{ forloop.index0 }}"
            data-non-pair-card
            data-pair-index="{{ forloop.index0 }}">
            <div class="non-pair-card__t">{{ pair.title }}</div>
            <p class="non-pair-card__b">{{ pair.body }}</p>
          </div>
        {%- endfor -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}


{%- if pf_ok -%}
  <script src="{{ 'pairing-triptych.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Product pairings",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading",
      "label": "Heading",
      "default": "Pour it with"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Appearance"
    },
    { "type": "color", "id": "bg_color", "label": "Background" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "text_color", "label": "Text colour" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "font_family",
      "label": "Font",
      "options": [
        { "value": "", "label": "Theme default" },
        { "value": "sans", "label": "Helvetica (all text)" },
        { "value": "mono", "label": "JetBrains Mono (all text)" }
      ],
      "default": ""
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "text_scale",
      "label": "Text size",
      "min": 70, "max": 150, "step": 5, "unit": "%", "default": 100,
      "info": "Scales every text size in this section together."
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "section_padding",
      "label": "Vertical padding",
      "min": 0, "max": 160, "step": 8, "unit": "px", "default": 0,
      "info": "0 keeps the section's own spacing."
    }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "pairing",
      "name": "Pairing",
      "settings": [
        { "type": "text", "id": "title", "label": "Title" },
        { "type": "textarea", "id": "body", "label": "Body" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "presets": [ { "name": "Product pairings" } ]
}
{% endschema %}
